25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:7=
00">Good morning, world. </span><span>When we moved to Cardiff and renovate=
d the ground floor of an old Victorian house, one thing I knew I wanted was=
 lots of glass in my kitchen. I liked the idea of lots of daylight, of seei=
ng the trees outside.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Our builder advised against it. Even=
 in Wales, hardly famous for tropical temperatures, it could become uncomfo=
rtably hot without air-conditioning, he warned. After last summer=E2=80=99s=
 heat wave, I=E2=80=99m glad I followed his advice.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I=E2=
=80=99m not the only one susceptible to the allure of glass. Around the wor=
ld, glass skyscrapers =E2=80=94 those glittering symbols of modernity =E2=
=80=94 are flying up at a rapid rate, often in places much hotter than Card=
iff. But the air-conditioning required to make these spaces usable is extre=
mely energy intensive. Today, my colleague Chico Harlan, our global climate=
 correspondent, writes on why some argue it=E2=80=99s time to rethink the g=
lass skyscraper.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;=
color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Also:</span></p><div style=3D"margin:0 au=
