r-spacing:.01em">Ulet Ifansasti for The New York Times</span></span></td></=
tr></tbody></table><h2 style=3D"color:#000;font:700 24px/31px nyt-cheltenha=
m,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>Shiny, modern and hot</span></h2><p =
style=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin=
:0 0 10px"><span>By Chico Harlan</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>More than any other form =
of architecture, glass skyscrapers testify to the human desire for sweeping=
 views and a sense of rising above the fray. In some of the world=E2=80=99s=
 fastest developing places =E2=80=94 Dubai, Mumbai, Jakarta, Kuala Lumpur =
=E2=80=94 they have become city-defining showpieces, signaling sophisticati=
on and rising financial clout.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px =
georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Those places also all happe=
n to get very hot.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Glass tends to be ill-suited for keepin=
g out heat. Its overuse often makes buildings more energy intensive to cool=
=2E One woman I talked to, who lives on the 74th floor of an apartment in K=
uala Lumpur, talked about the =E2=80=9Cwall of heat=E2=80=9D she felt emana=
ting from her windows on sunny days.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That relationship bet=
ween glass and heat is now fueling a debate about whether these symbols of =
modernity make sense in a world growing hotter because of climate change.</=
span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:=
0 0 15px"><span>Some argue that the ambition and innovation that spawned th=
e high-rise era can also develop technology to help modern skyscrapers fend=
 off rising temperatures while maintaining the million-dollar views.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>Others say these shiny behemoths are at odds with the goal of re=
ducing greenhouse-gas emissions. They say it is time to rethink city skylin=
es.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Natural light, equatorial he=
at</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;mar=
gin:0 0 15px"><span>In the spaces where we live and work, few amenities are=
 more desirable than natural light.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/=
25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But glass can make roo=
ms hotter in two ways. It lets in more heat from the air outside than solid=
 walls. It can also admit more than half of the energy from sunlight as hea=
t inside.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That means wrapping skyscrapers in glass can sig=
nificantly increase the need for air-conditioning when it=E2=80=99s warm or=
 sunny out. And some parts of the world are warmer and sunnier than others.=
</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D=
"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center"><img width=3D"500" =
height=3D"624" style=3D"display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:500px;heig=
ht:auto;padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:8px" class=3D"css-18ei5jy" src=3D"h=
ttps://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/10/30/multimedia/30int-theworld-glass-s=
kyscrapers-02-zmpv/30int-theworld-glass-skyscrapers-02-zmpv-jumbo.jpg" alt=
=3D"People on a sunny sidewalk in front of a modern building with a geometr=
ic glass facade. "></td></tr><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center;width:100%;=
padding-bottom:15px;line-height:14px" class=3D"css-1b9cqt1"><span style=3D"=
display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:500px;text-align:left" class=3D"cs=
s-14cus5q"><span style=3D"color:#666;font:normal 14px georgia,serif">The Gh=
erkin in London. =C2=A0</span><span style=3D"margin:0;font:11px/11px georgi=
a,serif;color:#888;letter-spacing:.01em">Joseph Horton for The New York Tim=
es</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In cities like New York an=
d London that have pioneered this style of architecture, glass can reduce h=
eating costs in the winter months. But the form has now spread across China=
, the Middle East and Southeast Asia =E2=80=94 sometimes in cities close to=
 the Equator that experience direct sunlight and intense heat year round.</=
span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:=
0 0 15px"><span>Over the past decade, the world has constructed 25 times th=
e number of buildings 200 meters or taller that it did in the 1980s. The =
=E2=80=9Cvast majority=E2=80=9D have facades largely of glass, one expert t=
old me.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#33=
3;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Malaysia, in particular, has gone on a building sp=
ree.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>The country started in 1996 by completing the Petrona=
s Towers, the tallest buildings in the world at the time. Then it kept goin=
g, constructing hundreds more skyscrapers. Malaysia now has more buildings =
200 meters or higher than any country but China, the United States and the =
United Arab Emirates.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Some of these buildings have good en=
ergy efficiency numbers. Proponents of glass-dominant design say these buil=
dings </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">can</span><span> be environm=
entally responsible, with the right choices: fins or undulations to prevent=
 sunlight hitting glass directly, for example, or metal coatings on the gla=
ss to reflect away radiation.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px g=
eorgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But the overall quality of M=
alaysia=E2=80=99s towers is =E2=80=9Cstill not so good,=E2=80=9D said Sarly=
 Adre Sarkum, an architect and the chief executive of Malaysia=E2=80=99s Gr=
een Building Index. He said that in Malaysia=E2=80=99s climate, extensive g=
lass facades carry =E2=80=9Csignificant energy consequences.=E2=80=9D</span=
></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">The air-conditioning trap</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span>The glass skyscraper became viable only with the advent of air-condit=
ioning. Today, critics of the approach say that technology effectively led =
architects to ignore other design lessons about contending with heat, inclu=
ding techniques honed over centuries, such as the white, sun-reflective fac=
ades of Greece or the open, traditional homes of Southeast Asia.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span>In Malaysia, the first modestly tall buildings looked as if they wer=
e in an almost defensive stance against the sun. The country=E2=80=99s 18-s=
tory Parliament building, built in the 1960s, came wrapped in a honeycomb-l=
ike lattice. Another early skyscraper, the Dayabumi Complex, built in the 1=
980s, had its windows shrouded behind an ornate white veil inspired by Isla=
mic motifs.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color=
:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I also visited a neighborhood called Kampung B=
aru, in the center of Kuala Lumpur. Surrounded by high rises, it is an isla=
nd of one- and two-story homes, preserved almost by accident because of col=
onial-era laws.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Today it is an architectural time capsule.=
 People hang out in open-air restaurants under ceiling fans, and many homes=
 have large overhangs that ward off the sun. Small windows are covered by h=
orizontal louvers.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>One of the oldest homes in the neighbor=
hood belongs to Jamiyah Kesot, 71. Constructed of dark timber with a high-p=
itched roof, it relies on the cooling features from a different time. It=E2=
